# C# Documentation Standards for Spellwright

Defines mandatory documentation standards for all C# scripts in the Spellwright project, following official C# XML documentation guidelines and Unity/DOTS best practices.

## Core Documentation Principles

### 1. XML Documentation Format

All public and internal types, members, and methods **MUST** use C# XML documentation comments with triple-slash `///` format.

**Format:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Brief description of the type or member.
/// </summary>
public class ExampleClass { }
```

**Never use:**

-   Single-line comments `//` for API documentation
-   Multi-line comments `/* */` for API documentation
-   Inline comments for public member descriptions

### 2. Mandatory Documentation Targets

**MUST be documented:**

-   ✅ All public classes, structs, and interfaces
-   ✅ All public and internal methods
-   ✅ All public properties and fields
-   ✅ All ECS components (IComponentData)
-   ✅ All ECS systems (ISystem, SystemBase)
-   ✅ All Jobs (IJob, IJobEntity)
-   ✅ All enum types and their values
-   ✅ All delegates and events

**Optional documentation:**

-   Private methods (recommended for complex logic)
-   Private fields (only if non-obvious purpose)

### 3. Documentation Completeness

**Minimum required tags:**

-   `<summary>` - Brief description (1-3 sentences)
-   `<param>` - For each method parameter
-   `<returns>` - For methods with non-void return
-   `<exception>` - For methods that can throw exceptions
-   `<remarks>` - For important implementation details (when needed)

## Standard XML Tags Reference

### Essential Tags

#### `<summary>`

Brief description of type or member. **Mandatory for all public APIs.**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Represents a projectile spell that moves in a straight line.
/// </summary>
public struct ProjectileComponent : IComponentData { }
```

**Rules:**

-   Keep it concise (1-3 sentences)
-   Start with active verbs: "Represents", "Defines", "Calculates", "Processes"
-   Avoid redundancy: Don't repeat the member name
-   Use present tense

**❌ Bad:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// ProjectileComponent
/// </summary>
```

**✅ Good:**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Defines movement data for projectile-type spell generators.
/// </summary>
```

#### `<param>`

Describes method parameters. **Mandatory for all parameters.**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Moves the entity by the specified offset.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="entity">The entity to move.</param>
/// <param name="offset">The movement offset in world space.</param>
public void MoveEntity(Entity entity, float3 offset) { }
```

**Rules:**

-   One `<param>` tag per parameter
-   Match parameter name exactly
-   Describe purpose and valid values
-   Start with "The" or "A/An"

#### `<returns>`

Describes return value. **Mandatory for non-void methods.**

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Calculates the damage after applying modifiers.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="baseDamage">The base damage value.</param>
/// <returns>The final damage value after all modifiers are applied.</returns>
public float CalculateDamage(float baseDamage) { }
```

**Rules:**

-   Describe what is returned, not just the type
-   Include range or possible values when relevant
-   Mention null conditions for reference types

#### `<exception>`

Documents exceptions that can be thrown.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Retrieves a spell component from the entity.
/// </summary>
/// <param name="entity">The target entity.</param>
/// <returns>The spell component data.</returns>
/// <exception cref="System.InvalidOperationException">
/// Thrown when the entity does not have a spell component.
/// </exception>
public SpellData GetSpellData(Entity entity) { }
```

### Formatting Tags

#### `<c>` - Inline Code

For inline code terms within text.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Sets the <c>Lifetime</c> component to the specified duration.
/// </summary>
```

#### `<code>` - Code Block

For multi-line code examples.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Creates a new projectile spell.
/// </summary>
/// <example>
/// <code>
/// var spell = CreateProjectile();
/// spell.Speed = 10f;
/// spell.Lifetime = 5f;
/// </code>
/// </example>
```

#### `<para>` - Paragraph

For structured text with multiple paragraphs.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Handles spell collision detection.
/// <para>
/// This system uses Unity Physics to detect collisions between spell entities
/// and target entities.
/// </para>
/// <para>
/// Collision results are processed in the same frame to ensure determinism.
/// </para>
/// </summary>
```

### Cross-Reference Tags

#### `<see cref=""/>` - Inline Reference

Reference to another code element.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Applies damage to the target. See also <see cref="HealSystem"/> for healing.
/// </summary>
```

**Rules:**

-   Use fully qualified names when ambiguous: `<see cref="UnityEngine.Transform"/>`
-   For generics, use curly braces: `<see cref="DynamicBuffer{T}"/>`
-   Works with: classes, methods, properties, fields, enums

#### `<seealso cref=""/>` - Related Links

Links to related code elements.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Processes projectile movement.
/// </summary>
/// <seealso cref="HomingModifierSystem"/>
/// <seealso cref="PierceModifierSystem"/>
public partial struct ProjectileMovementSystem : ISystem { }
```

### Additional Tags

#### `<example>` - Usage Example

Provides usage examples.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Bakes spell JSON into ECS components.
/// </summary>
/// <example>
/// This example shows how to bake a projectile spell:
/// <code>
/// var json = "{ \"generator\": \"projectile\", \"speed\": 15 }";
/// BakeSpellFromJson(entity, json);
/// </code>
/// </example>
```

#### `<remarks>` - Implementation Details

Additional important information.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Updates spell lifetimes and destroys expired entities.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// This system runs in the SimulationSystemGroup after physics updates.
/// All lifetime values are in seconds and use Time.DeltaTime for updates.
/// Performance: O(n) where n is the number of entities with Lifetime component.
/// </remarks>
public partial struct LifetimeSystem : ISystem { }
```

#### `<value>` - Property Description

Describes property value and behavior.

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the spell's movement speed.
/// </summary>
/// <value>
/// The speed in units per second. Must be non-negative.
/// Default value is 10.0f.
/// </value>
public float Speed { get; set; }
```

## Unity-Specific Documentation Patterns

### MonoBehaviour and ScriptableObject

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Handles player input for spell casting.
/// Attach this component to the player GameObject.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Requires: PlayerController component
/// Unity Messages: Update(), OnEnable(), OnDisable()
/// </remarks>
public class SpellCastingInput : MonoBehaviour
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The key code used to cast the primary spell.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Configure this in the Unity Inspector. Default: Mouse0
    /// </remarks>
    [SerializeField]
    private KeyCode castKey = KeyCode.Mouse0;
}
```

**Rules:**

-   Document Unity Inspector fields with `<remarks>` for configuration notes
-   Mention required components and dependencies
-   List Unity messages (Awake, Start, Update, etc.) if not obvious

### ECS Components (IComponentData)

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Defines the remaining lifetime of a spell entity.
/// When lifetime reaches zero, the entity is destroyed.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// This is an unmanaged component compatible with Burst compilation.
/// Updated by: LifetimeSystem
/// Performance: Blittable type, cache-friendly
/// </remarks>
public struct Lifetime : IComponentData
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The remaining time in seconds before the entity is destroyed.
    /// </summary>
    /// <value>
    /// Time in seconds. Must be non-negative.
    /// Set to negative value to indicate unlimited lifetime.
    /// </value>
    public float Value;
}
```

**Required information:**

-   What the component represents
-   Which systems read/write it
-   Performance characteristics (blittable, burst-compatible)
-   Data constraints and valid ranges

### ECS Systems (ISystem)

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Processes projectile movement for all spell entities with ProjectileComponent.
/// Updates entity positions based on velocity and delta time.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// System Group: SimulationSystemGroup
/// Update Order: After TransformSystemGroup
/// Burst Compiled: Yes
/// Threading: Parallel (IJobEntity)
/// Performance: O(n) where n = number of projectile entities
/// </remarks>
[BurstCompile]
[UpdateInGroup(typeof(SimulationSystemGroup))]
[UpdateAfter(typeof(TransformSystemGroup))]
public partial struct ProjectileMovementSystem : ISystem
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Called when the system is created.
    /// Initializes system state and queries.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="state">The system state provided by Unity.</param>
    public void OnCreate(ref SystemState state) { }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called every frame to update projectile positions.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="state">The system state provided by Unity.</param>
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state) { }
}
```

**Required information:**

-   What the system does (verb: "Processes", "Updates", "Handles")
-   Which components it queries
-   System group and update order
-   Burst compilation status
-   Threading model (parallel/sequential)
-   Performance characteristics

### ECS Jobs (IJobEntity)

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Parallel job that updates projectile positions based on velocity.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Burst Compiled: Yes
/// Schedule Mode: Parallel
/// Components: Read/Write LocalTransform, Read Velocity
/// </remarks>
[BurstCompile]
public partial struct MoveProjectilesJob : IJobEntity
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The delta time for this frame, in seconds.
    /// </summary>
    public float DeltaTime;

    /// <summary>
    /// Executes the job for a single entity.
    /// Updates the entity's position by applying velocity over delta time.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="transform">The entity's transform component.</param>
    /// <param name="velocity">The entity's velocity component.</param>
    public void Execute(ref LocalTransform transform, in Velocity velocity)
    {
        transform.Position += velocity.Value * DeltaTime;
    }
}
```

**Required information:**

-   Job purpose and execution model
-   Burst compilation status
-   Component access (Read/Write/Read-only)
-   Thread safety considerations

## Spellwright Architecture Documentation

### Generator/Modifier/Payload Pattern

Document how components fit into the spell architecture:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Projectile generator component for the Spellwright spell system.
/// Part of the Generator layer - defines how spells originate and move.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Architecture Layer: Generator (Mandatory - one per spell)
/// Compatible Modifiers: Homing, Pierce, Fork, Chain
/// Compatible Payloads: All payload types
/// Performance: Blittable component, Burst-compatible
/// </remarks>
public struct ProjectileComponent : IComponentData
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The movement speed of the projectile in units per second.
    /// </summary>
    /// <value>
    /// Speed in units/second. Valid range: 0.1f to 100.0f.
    /// Recommended range for balanced gameplay: 5.0f to 30.0f.
    /// </value>
    public float Speed;
}
```

### JSON Baking Systems

Document relationship to JSON schema:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Bakes projectile generator JSON into ECS components.
/// Converts JSON string data into optimized runtime ECS entities.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// System Type: Baking System (SystemBase)
/// Execution: One-time during spell creation
/// JSON Schema: Matches "generator.projectile" schema
/// Output Components: ProjectileComponent, Velocity, LocalTransform
/// Validation: Client-side JSON validation before baking
/// </remarks>
public partial class ProjectileBakingSystem : SystemBase
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Bakes a projectile spell from validated JSON data.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="entity">The target entity to add components to.</param>
    /// <param name="jsonData">The validated JSON object containing spell data.</param>
    /// <exception cref="System.ArgumentNullException">
    /// Thrown when jsonData is null.
    /// </exception>
    /// <exception cref="System.FormatException">
    /// Thrown when JSON structure doesn't match expected schema.
    /// </exception>
    protected override void OnUpdate() { }
}
```

## Performance Documentation

Always document performance characteristics for DOTS code:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// System that processes spell collisions using Unity Physics.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Performance Characteristics:
/// - Time Complexity: O(n*m) where n=spells, m=average hits per spell
/// - Memory: Zero GC allocations (uses NativeArrays)
/// - Burst Compiled: Yes (full Burst compatibility)
/// - Threading: Parallel via IJobEntity
/// - Cache Friendly: Sequential component access pattern
/// Target Performance: &lt;0.5ms for 1000 active spells @60fps
/// Profiling Category: "Spell.Physics"
/// </remarks>
```

**Include when relevant:**

-   Time complexity (Big O notation)
-   Memory allocation characteristics (zero GC)
-   Burst compilation status
-   Threading model
-   Cache efficiency
-   Target performance metrics
-   Unity Profiler category

## Validation and Constraints

Document data validation and constraints:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Validates spell JSON against the Spellwright schema.
/// Implements three-layer validation strategy.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Validation Layers:
/// 1. API Function Calling (LLM-side)
/// 2. Few-Shot Prompting (LLM-side)
/// 3. Client-side validation (This implementation)
/// </remarks>
public class SpellValidator
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Validates that damage value is within acceptable range.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="damage">The damage value to validate.</param>
    /// <returns>True if damage is valid; otherwise, false.</returns>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Valid Range: 1.0f to 1000.0f
    /// Recommended Range: 10.0f to 100.0f for balanced gameplay
    /// Validation Rule: Must be positive and finite
    /// </remarks>
    public bool ValidateDamage(float damage) { }
}
```

## Thread Safety Documentation

For systems that use jobs or parallel processing:

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Processes multiple spells in parallel using job system.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Thread Safety:
/// - Read-Only Access: Velocity, ProjectileComponent
/// - Write Access: LocalTransform (no shared writes)
/// - Parallel Safe: Yes (no race conditions)
/// - EntityCommandBuffer: Used for structural changes (deferred execution)
/// Synchronization: Implicit via IJobEntity scheduling
/// </remarks>
```

## Documentation Quality Checklist

Before committing code, verify:

-   [ ] All public types have `<summary>` tags
-   [ ] All method parameters have `<param>` tags
-   [ ] All non-void methods have `<returns>` tags
-   [ ] All exceptions are documented with `<exception>` tags
-   [ ] Cross-references use `<see cref=""/>` with correct syntax
-   [ ] ECS components document which systems use them
-   [ ] ECS systems document update order and burst compilation
-   [ ] Performance-critical code includes performance remarks
-   [ ] Unity-specific attributes are explained
-   [ ] No spelling or grammar errors in documentation

## Tools and Validation

### IDE Integration

-   Visual Studio: Enable XML documentation warnings (CS1591)
-   Rider: Enable "Missing XML comment" inspections
-   VS Code: Install C# XML Documentation extension

### Build-Time Validation

Documentation warnings should be treated as errors in CI/CD pipeline.

### AI-Assisted Documentation

When using AI to generate documentation:

-   Always review for technical accuracy
-   Verify code references are correct
-   Ensure performance claims are validated
-   Check Unity/DOTS terminology is correct

## Examples by Component Type

### Complete Component Example

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Defines homing behavior for spell projectiles.
/// Automatically tracks and pursues the nearest valid target within range.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// Architecture Layer: Modifier (Optional)
/// Compatible Generators: Projectile, Area of Effect
/// System: Processed by HomingModifierSystem
/// Performance: Blittable, Burst-compatible
/// Target Selection: Uses spatial hash for O(log n) nearest-neighbor search
/// </remarks>
public struct HomingModifier : IComponentData
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The maximum distance at which targets can be detected.
    /// </summary>
    /// <value>
    /// Detection range in world units. Valid range: 1.0f to 50.0f.
    /// Default: 15.0f units.
    /// </value>
    public float DetectionRange;

    /// <summary>
    /// The rotation speed when turning toward target.
    /// </summary>
    /// <value>
    /// Rotation speed in degrees per second. Valid range: 30.0f to 360.0f.
    /// Higher values create sharper turns.
    /// </value>
    public float TurnSpeed;

    /// <summary>
    /// The entity currently being tracked, or Entity.Null if no target.
    /// </summary>
    /// <value>
    /// The target entity. Set to Entity.Null when no valid target exists.
    /// Updated every frame by HomingModifierSystem.
    /// </value>
    public Entity Target;
}
```

### Complete System Example

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Updates the lifetime of all spell entities and destroys expired ones.
/// Runs every frame to decrease lifetime values and schedule destruction.
/// </summary>
/// <remarks>
/// System Group: SimulationSystemGroup
/// Update Order: Before TransformSystemGroup (early cleanup)
/// Burst Compiled: Yes
/// Threading: Parallel via IJobEntity for lifetime updates
/// Performance: O(n) where n = entities with Lifetime component
/// Target: &lt;0.1ms for 5000 entities @60fps
/// Entity Destruction: Deferred via EntityCommandBuffer
/// </remarks>
[BurstCompile]
[UpdateInGroup(typeof(SimulationSystemGroup))]
[UpdateBefore(typeof(TransformSystemGroup))]
public partial struct LifetimeSystem : ISystem
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Called when the system is created. Initializes entity queries.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="state">The system state provided by Unity ECS.</param>
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnCreate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Query entities with Lifetime component
        state.RequireForUpdate<Lifetime>();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Called every frame to update lifetimes and destroy expired entities.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="state">The system state provided by Unity ECS.</param>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Process:
    /// 1. Schedule parallel job to decrease all lifetime values
    /// 2. Query for expired entities (lifetime &lt;= 0)
    /// 3. Schedule destruction via EntityCommandBuffer
    /// 4. Playback command buffer at end of frame
    /// </remarks>
    [BurstCompile]
    public void OnUpdate(ref SystemState state)
    {
        // Implementation
    }
}
```

## Anti-Patterns to Avoid

### ❌ Don't: Redundant Documentation

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the speed.
/// </summary>
public float Speed { get; set; } // Adds no information
```

### ✅ Do: Meaningful Documentation

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Gets or sets the projectile movement speed in units per second.
/// </summary>
/// <value>Valid range: 0.1f to 100.0f. Default: 10.0f.</value>
public float Speed { get; set; }
```

### ❌ Don't: Incorrect Type References

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Uses List<int> for storage. // Invalid XML
/// </summary>
```

### ✅ Do: Proper XML Escaping

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Uses <see cref="System.Collections.Generic.List{T}"/> for storage.
/// </summary>
```

### ❌ Don't: Vague Descriptions

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Does stuff with the entity.
/// </summary>
```

### ✅ Do: Specific Descriptions

```csharp
/// <summary>
/// Applies fire damage to the target entity and starts burn status effect.
/// </summary>
```

## Important Notes

-   Documentation is **mandatory**, not optional
-   Write for developers who are unfamiliar with the code
-   Keep documentation synchronized with code changes
-   Document **why**, not just **what** - explain design decisions
-   Use proper XML syntax - validate with IDE tools
-   Performance documentation is critical for DOTS code
-   All documentation must be in **English**
-   Document public API surface area completely
-   Private implementation details are optional but encouraged
-   Update documentation when refactoring code
